WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.158
SOC 2 readiness depends on control owners and evidence.

2
00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:11.549
A control owner is responsible for understanding what the control
requires, how often it must operate,

3
00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:19.865
what systems or teams it covers, what evidence proves operation, and what
to do when the control does

4
00:00:19.865 --> 00:00:21.712
not operate as expected.

5
00:00:21.712 --> 00:00:26.332
Without ownership, controls become promises without a reliable operating
path.

6
00:00:26.332 --> 00:00:30.028
Evidence should be accurate, complete, timely, and traceable.

7
00:00:30.028 --> 00:00:36.033
It should show what happened, when it happened, who performed or reviewed
it,

8
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:41.115
which system or population was covered, and how exceptions were handled.

9
00:00:41.115 --> 00:00:47.121
Evidence does not need to be beautiful, but it needs to be trustworthy.

10
00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:54.974
A messy but authentic ticket trail is better than a polished document that
does not reflect reality.

11
00:00:54.974 --> 00:01:02.828
Common evidence includes access review records, onboarding and offboarding
tickets, change approvals, pull request records, deployment tickets,

12
00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:10.681
policy acknowledgments, security training completion, risk assessments,
vendor reviews, backup test results, monitoring alerts, incident response
records,

13
00:01:10.681 --> 00:01:16.225
vulnerability remediation records, configuration screenshots, and meeting
notes where decisions are documented.

14
00:01:16.225 --> 00:01:21.768
The exact evidence depends on the control, scope, auditor, and review
period.

15
00:01:21.768 --> 00:01:25.002
Type II readiness requires recurring evidence collection.

16
00:01:25.002 --> 00:01:29.622
If a control operates monthly, evidence should be collected monthly.

17
00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:36.089
If a control operates when a change occurs, evidence should be tied to
changes.

18
00:01:36.089 --> 00:01:43.019
If a control operates during incidents, the team should preserve incident
records when incidents happen.

19
00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:51.334
Waiting until the end of the period and trying to reconstruct months of
activity creates risk and stress.

20
00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:56.416
Engineering, security, IT, HR, operations, and vendor-management teams
often contribute evidence.

21
00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:59.649
HR may own onboarding and offboarding records.

22
00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:02.421
Engineering may own change management evidence.

23
00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:09.813
Security may own monitoring, incidents, vulnerability management, and
access review coordination. Operations may own backup tests.

24
00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:12.585
Vendor management may own third-party reviews.

25
00:02:12.585 --> 00:02:16.742
SOC 2 is cross-functional because real controls are cross-functional.

26
00:02:16.742 --> 00:02:21.824
The ethical rule is simple: do not fabricate or misrepresent evidence.

27
00:02:21.824 --> 00:02:29.215
Do not create fake logs, hide failed controls, backdate approvals, or
remove exceptions to look cleaner.

28
00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:37.531
If a gap exists, document it, evaluate impact, remediate it, and improve
the process. Honest evidence supports trust.

29
00:02:37.531 --> 00:02:41.226
Misleading evidence defeats the purpose of the report.
